bridge/codex/ — talking to `codex app-server`
=============================================

Tested Codex versions: >=0.172.0 <0.173.0

That range is also TESTED in protocol.js, and test/codex-rpc.test.js fails when
the two disagree. A Codex outside it may work; it has not been looked at. The
plan (docs/plans/23-codex.md) has the bridge show a banner in that case rather
than refuse.

What is here (Phase 0 of the plan — nothing in the bridge calls it yet):

  rpc.js       JSON-RPC 2.0 over a readable and a writable. Newline-delimited
               JSON, an id → promise map, a handler table for server requests,
               and log-once-and-drop for notifications this build does not know.
               Knows nothing about processes, so it works over the session
               host's relayed socket as well as over a child's stdio.
  client.js    Spawns `codex app-server --listen stdio://` (CODEX_BIN, which is
               TGXCODE_CODEX_BIN or `codex`) and does the initialize /
               initialized handshake with experimentalApi on.
  protocol.js  The tested range, and the ServerNotification / ServerRequest
               method lists copied from the schema for that version.

Only Node built-ins, like everything else under bridge/.

Upgrading Codex
---------------

1. `npm i -g @openai/codex@<new>` and `codex --version`.
2. Regenerate the schema beside the old one, both variants:

     codex app-server generate-json-schema --experimental --out /tmp/s-exp
     codex app-server generate-json-schema --out /tmp/s-stable
     mkdir -p docs/codex-schema/<new>/{experimental,stable}
     cp /tmp/s-exp/codex_app_server_protocol.schemas.json    docs/codex-schema/<new>/experimental/
     cp /tmp/s-stable/codex_app_server_protocol.schemas.json docs/codex-schema/<new>/stable/

   Only the combined bundle is committed. The generator also writes one file
   per type (about 6 MB more), all of which is in the bundle.
3. `diff` the two versions' bundles. Renamed or removed methods are the ones
   that break us; new notifications are harmless until we want them.
4. Update TESTED and the two lists in protocol.js, and the range above.
5. Re-record the fixtures (test/fixtures/codex/record.js --list), read them
   before committing (the repo is public; the recorder scrubs what it knows
   about), and run `node test/codex-rpc.test.js`.
6. Re-check the plan's *Measured on* table, and move what changed into it.

Fit with the runtime contract
-----------------------------

Phase 1 put the process contract in the header of
bridge/runtimes/claude/process.js. Checked against it on 0.172.0, the
Codex adapter (Phase 3) can be written inside it except where noted.

Fits as it is:

  - Framing. The runner splits lines and parses them; Codex is
    newline-delimited JSON both ways. So the adapter takes parsed objects,
    and rpc.js's stream handling is not used for a conversation.
  - Correlated requests. The runner mints string ids (`req_<tag>_<n>`), and
    Codex's RequestId is `string | integer`. `request(id, verb, payload)` is
    `{id, method: verb, params: payload}`, and a message with `id` and no
    `method` is a `response` event.
  - Unknown messages give an empty event list. Use protocol.js's
    KNOWN_NOTIFICATIONS for "unknown" and log once, as rpc.js does.
  - Server requests are asks: requestApproval becomes `ask`, and
    `serverRequest/resolved` becomes `ask-withdrawn`, but only for an ask still
    unanswered, because Codex sends it after every answer. Anything not
    handled becomes `request`, and `refuse()` writes a -32601.
  - Lifecycle. `turn/start` and `turn/steer` take `clientUserMessageId`, and
    the userMessage item has a `clientId` field, so `lifecycle` should be able
    to name the runner's `uuid`. This is unmeasured: the fixtures send no
    clientUserMessageId, and `clientId` is null in all of them.
  - setPermissionMode needs no request; it settles at once and the adapter
    sends the new settings on the next turn/start (D5). withdraw() is always
    false, since Codex cannot take back a steered message.

Needs a contract change:

  1. The handshake has to gate the queue. The runner calls handshake(),
     ignores a rejection and flushes the queue straight away. Codex refuses
     everything before `initialize` with -32600 "Not initialized", and a turn
     also needs `thread/start` or `thread/resume` answered first, because
     turn/start needs the threadId. The runner should wait for handshake() to
     settle before its first flush. Claude's resolves at once, so it loses
     nothing. For Codex a rejection is fatal, not "an older build".
  2. A new session's id is minted by Codex. The thread id comes back from
     thread/start, and the runner reads an `init` whose sessionId differs from
     its own as a fork (it emits `forked`). The contract needs a way to say
     "this is the id of a new session": an `init` field, or an isNew rule in
     the runner.
  3. Nothing sends `init` on its own. Claude prints system/init when it
     starts; Codex says nothing until asked. The adapter has to build `init`
     from the initialize and thread/start responses. It can, but those are
     responses to requests the adapter made, not the runner (see 4).
  4. turn() returns a line, but turn/start and turn/steer are requests that
     need an id, and their answers carry the turn id that interrupt() and the
     next steer need. The adapter can mint ids from its own space (say
     `codex_<n>`) and keep those responses to itself, with no `response`
     event. That works only if the runner ignores a response it is not
     waiting on, and the contract should say it does.
  5. A refused steer has to be sent again, not dropped. A message handed over
     just as the turn ends gets -32600 "no active turn to steer". The
     `lifecycle` states either keep a turn or drop it (`cancelled`,
     `discarded`), and read() cannot write. So the contract needs a state, or
     an event, meaning "not taken: queue it again as a turn of its own".
  6. answer() is too narrow for Codex's decisions. `{behavior, allowSession}`
     covers `accept` and `decline`. A command request's `availableDecisions`
     can instead hold `{acceptWithExecpolicyAmendment}`, a persistent rule,
     and `cancel`, which also ends the turn. The ask needs to carry the list,
     and the answer a decision chosen from it.
  7. requestUserInput is answered with `{answers: {<id>: {answers: [...]}}}`
     and does not fit `{behavior, updatedInput}` cleanly. Treat it like (6),
     an answer shape per ask kind, rather than squeezing it into updatedInput.
